-- WHO COMPETES, AND NEVER IN SILENCE (#1143, operator 2026-11-21): the first-write-wins rule of layers (docs/c1f-layers.md) is between SIBLINGS, the packs that stack on one class. A write from a pack that
-- is not one of that class's layers (the door's `entity_write` is a setter of the `door_tools` pack) is an INPUT to the class, like a person's write: it claims nothing, and the class's own setters answer it
-- normally (a setter may rewrite the property that fired it). When siblings really do compete and the skipped write is a setter rewriting its OWN trigger, the skip is not quiet: the entity carries a
-- `refused` receipt that names the setter and the pack that won. Found on a seat: Memo's `fix` setter rewrote `a` and was skipped for hours because the door pack had written `a` first.
-- Converted from tests/loud_guard_1143.rs. `trig` is loaded as the FIRST app (the root of trust), as `door_tools` is: a privileged pack may write any class.

test a write from a pack that is not a layer of the class is an input and the classs setter may rewrite it
list trig.packs .
load probe2.c1f
given Probe2:y {"a": "idle"}
given Trig:t {"target": "Probe2:y", "tprop": "a"}
write Trig:t.go = 1
-- the `trig` pack wrote `a` (an input); `fix`, a layer of Probe2, rewrote it
reads Probe2:y.a = "owed"
-- nothing was refused
reads Probe2:y.refused = null

test when siblings compete a skipped rewrite of the setters own trigger leaves a receipt naming both
load lower.c1f
load upper.c1f
given Probe5:y {"a": "idle"}
write Probe5:y.a = "open"
eval 'lowfix' in str(Probe5:y.refused) = true
eval 'upper' in str(Probe5:y.refused) = true
